Subject: Specifying priors on Intercept and Slope mean in LGCM
Message-ID: <6U-EbJ7wXAY.-2ODCikOAQAJ@blavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/blavaan/c/6U-EbJ7wXAY/m/-2ODCikOAQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all,

I’m running a Bayesian latent growth model, and I would like to check whether I have specified the priors in the correct way.

I have specified the (blavaan default) priors for mean of the Intercept and Slope as follows:

i ~ prior("dnorm(0, 0.001)") * 1

s ~ prior("dnorm(0, 0.001)") * 1

However, when I specify the priors like this, the prior distributions of other parameters in the model change...

For example, the Wishart distribution of the Covariance Int & Slope changes into a beta distribution, and the variances of the Intercept and Slope become a gamma distribution instead of a Wishart (looks like they are treated as residual variances)...

Does anyone know how these priors should be specified?

Best,

Sanne

From u6ee88ef185ee97d5 at groups.google.com  Sat Nov 18 16:39:23 2017
From: u6ee88ef185ee97d5 at groups.google.com (Paolo Toff)
Date: Sat, 18 Nov 2017 16:39:23 +0000
Subject: Discrepancy in model syntax between lavaan and blavaan
Message-ID: <HrHbP4jhqaA.pm3Pn2dEAQAJ@blavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/blavaan/c/HrHbP4jhqaA/m/pm3Pn2dEAQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear group,

I found a discrepancy between the model syntaxes used by lavaan and blavaan.
When specifying a multiple mediators model in lavaan I use to recycle the parameters defined by the ':=' operator.
For example:
indirectEffect := a*b
total := c + (a*b)
can also be written:
indirectEffect := a*b
total := c + (indirectEffect)
In my opinion, The second type of syntax with the recycling of the parameter, makes the specification of the model syntax more intuitive. However, it generates an error in blavaan that says something like:
---------
Error : The following error occured when compiling and adapting the model using rjags:
 Error in rjags::jags.model(model, data = dataenv, inits = inits, n.chains = length(runjags.object$end.state),  :

Error parsing model file:
syntax error on line 34 near "t1"

It may help to use failed.jags(c('model','data','inits')) to see model/data/inits syntax with line numbers
Error in blavaan(modelCompact, data = Data, burnin = 4000, sample = 10000,  :
  blavaan ERROR: problem with MCMC estimation.  The model syntax and data have been exported.
-----
Since avoiding the second type of syntax prevents the error as well, I am posting it in the hope to help someone else in avoiding the error if s/he also specifies the syntax in the way I did.

Anyway, blavaan and lavaan are great and thank you guys for creating, developing, and making them available to us.

From u645e2b041ea2a63e at groups.google.com  Mon Nov 20 04:18:39 2017
From: u645e2b041ea2a63e at groups.google.com (Ed Merkle)
Date: Mon, 20 Nov 2017 04:18:39 +0000
Subject: Re: Specifying priors on Intercept and Slope mean in LGCM
Message-ID: <6U-EbJ7wXAY.B5Fbq_RZAwAJ@blavaan.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <6U-EbJ7wXAY.-2ODCikOAQAJ@blavaan.groups.google.com>
References: <6U-EbJ7wXAY.-2ODCikOAQAJ@blavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/blavaan/c/6U-EbJ7wXAY/m/B5Fbq_RZAwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Sanne,

In jags, it can be helpful to jointly put an inverse wishart on variance/covariance parameters associated with latent variables. However, when you start to put custom priors on latent variable parameters, it can become impossible to use an inverse wishart. So blavaan checks whether you are using your own custom priors on latent variable parameters, then decides what to do about this prior.

The blavaan paper has lots of detail on custom specification of these priors. Basically, if you don't use an inverse wishart, you can write each covariance as the product of a correlation and two standard deviations. Then the priors go on the correlation (beta) and associated variances (inverse gamma).

Either of these specifications (inverse wishart or correlation/variances) is valid. The sampling under inverse wishart might be a little more efficient, but it will not make a major difference in many situations.

Best,
Ed

